<div class="ed-reading guide-reading text-lg">
<p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">A password manager can make unique passwords easier to use, but it is not a guarantee against every account problem. The useful starting point is a maintained product that works with your devices, supports the sign-in features you need and gives you a recovery path you understand.</p>
<section class="ed-start" id="start" aria-labelledby="start-heading"><p class="ed-label">Start here</p><h2 id="start-heading">Start with the system around the password</h2><p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">Choose for practical fit rather than a brand ranking. Check the devices, browsers and phones you actually use before moving anything important.</p><div class="ed-choices">
{{ choice("Check device support", "Confirm that the manager supports your computer, phone, browser and the sign-in methods you use.", "#choose-a-maintained-fit", "startup", "/img/guides/password-field-guide-v1.webp", "A closed password vault beside a key.", "Read this part") }}
{{ choice("Create one strong master passphrase", "Make it long, unique and unpredictable. Do not base it on personal information or reuse it elsewhere.", "#protect-the-manager", "applications", "/img/guides/password-field-guide-v1.webp", "A browser card and phone with abstract masked fields.", "Read this part") }}
{{ choice("Practise before expanding", "Save, find and use one ordinary low-risk account at its known correct website.", "#make-the-first-sign-in", "online", "/img/guides/password-field-guide-v1.webp", "A sealed recovery envelope beside a notebook.", "Read this part") }}
</div></section>
<h2 class="ed-section-heading" id="choose-a-maintained-fit"><span aria-hidden="true">01</span>Choose a maintained fit</h2>
<p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">Look for a product that is actively maintained, works on your supported devices and offers the everyday features you need, such as synchronisation and autofill. Check whether multi-factor authentication is available for the manager account and whether the recovery options are suitable for you.</p><p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">Do not choose only because a product is popular or inexpensive. A system that does not work on your phone, browser or computer may leave you bypassing it when you are in a hurry. Read the provider's current device and account documentation before committing.</p><p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">Useful starting information is available from <a class="font-semibold text-accent-ink underline underline-offset-4 hover:text-ink" href="https://www.cyber.gov.au/protect-yourself/securing-your-accounts/password-managers">Cyber.gov.au's password manager guidance</a>.</p>
<h2 class="ed-section-heading" id="protect-the-manager"><span aria-hidden="true">02</span>Protect the manager itself</h2>
<p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">Your master passphrase protects access to the manager, so treat it differently from the individual passwords stored inside. Make it long, unique and difficult to predict. Avoid names, dates, addresses and other personal details. Never reuse it for email, shopping or another service.</p><p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">Multi-factor authentication can add another type of check, such as an authenticator code or security key. It improves protection but does not remove the need to protect your devices and recovery material.</p><p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">Keep the manager signed in only on devices you trust and can lock. A lost master passphrase may not be recoverable, so identify the provider's official recovery choices before you need them. See <a class="font-semibold text-accent-ink underline underline-offset-4 hover:text-ink" href="https://www.cyber.gov.au/protect-yourself/securing-your-accounts/passphrases">passphrase guidance</a> and <a class="font-semibold text-accent-ink underline underline-offset-4 hover:text-ink" href="https://www.cyber.gov.au/learn-basics/explore-basics/mfa">MFA guidance</a>.</p>
<h2 class="ed-section-heading" id="make-the-first-sign-in"><span aria-hidden="true">03</span>Make one careful first sign-in</h2>
<p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">Begin with an ordinary, low-risk account rather than banking, government or another account where a mistake could interrupt something important. Open the website through a known bookmark or address, check the domain, and sign in without rushing.</p><p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">When creating a new password, make sure the manager saves it and that the website accepts it before closing your existing access. Then sign out and practise finding the entry and signing in again. Repeat the ordinary sign-in check on each supported device you rely on; a browser check alone does not verify the phone.</p>{% call note("care", "Take care", "Do not test on a critical account first") %}<p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">Learning the basic flow on a low-risk account reduces the chance of locking yourself out while you are still working out the app, browser or recovery process.</p>{% endcall %}
